<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Athens</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Athens is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> that was a leading city-state of the classical era. It played a central role in resisting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a>, including the victory at Marathon in 490 BCE. After the closure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonist</a> school in Athens in the sixth century, some of its philosophers took refuge at the court of the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athens.jpg" alt="Athens" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Parlette · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_Acropolis_at_Daybreak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
